    sampleAnswer: "Start with the target audience and the problem the product solves for them, then define the primary business goal (awareness, leads, direct sales) and a small set of measurable KPIs tied to that goal. From there, map the customer journey and select channels that match where the audience actually spends attention and how they research or buy ? rather than defaulting to whichever channels are most familiar. Launch with a smaller, testable set of channels and messages, measure results against the defined KPIs, and reallocate budget toward what's actually working rather than spreading effort evenly across everything from day one.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nStart with the target audience and the problem the product solves for them, then define the primary business goal (awareness, leads, direct sales) and a small set of measurable KPIs tied to that goal. From there, map the customer journey and select channels that match where the audience actually spends attention and how they research or buy ? rather than defaulting to whichever channels are most familiar. Launch with a smaller, testable set of channels and messages, measure results against the defined KPIs, and reallocate budget toward what's actually working rather than spreading effort evenly across everything from day one.\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor a new budget-friendly meal-kit product targeting busy young professionals, a strategy might start with Instagram/TikTok ads and influencer partnerships (where the audience actually spends time) plus SEO content around quick-meal planning, rather than starting with, say, a broad TV-style display campaign that doesn't match how this audience discovers new products.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\nSEO\n\n",
