    shortDescription: "On-page SEO covers everything controlled directly on the page itself: content quality and relevance to search intent, title tags and meta descriptions, header structure, internal l...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you understand how search visibility and traffic acquisition work in practice, rather than relying on isolated SEO terminology.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- Explain the concept accurately and in terms of search intent or relevance.\n- Recognize the practical trade-offs and common implementation mistakes.\n- Connect the tactic to a realistic website or campaign decision.",
    sampleAnswer: "On-page SEO covers everything controlled directly on the page itself: content quality and relevance to search intent, title tags and meta descriptions, header structure, internal linking, page speed, mobile-friendliness, and structured data. Off-page SEO covers signals earned outside the page, primarily backlinks from other reputable sites (which search engines treat as a vote of trust and relevance), as well as brand mentions and, to some extent, social signals. Both matter together ? strong on-page optimization with no external authority signals rarely ranks well for competitive terms, and a page with strong backlinks but poor content and user experience will still tend to underperform once it's found.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nOn-page SEO covers everything controlled directly on the page itself: content quality and relevance to search intent, title tags and meta descriptions, header structure, internal linking, page speed, mobile-friendliness, and structured data. Off-page SEO covers signals earned outside the page, primarily backlinks from other reputable sites (which search engines treat as a vote of trust and relevance), as well as brand mentions and, to some extent, social signals. Both matter together ? strong on-page optimization with no external authority signals rarely ranks well for competitive terms, and a page with strong backlinks but poor content and user experience will still tend to underperform once it's found.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA well-optimized blog post (clear structure, fast-loading, directly answering the search query) that also earns links from a handful of relevant industry sites will typically outrank a similarly well-written post with no external links pointing to it, for a competitive keyword.",
