    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you understand how search visibility and traffic acquisition work in practice, rather than relying on isolated SEO terminology.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- Explain the concept accurately and in terms of search intent or relevance.\n- Recognize the practical trade-offs and common implementation mistakes.\n- Connect the tactic to a realistic website or campaign decision.",
    sampleAnswer: "Quality Score is Google Ads' estimate (on a 1-10 scale, shown at the keyword level) of how relevant and useful an ad, keyword, and landing page combination is likely to be to a searcher. It's influenced primarily by expected click-through rate, ad relevance to the keyword, and landing page experience (load speed, relevance, and usability). A higher Quality Score generally means a lower cost-per-click for the same ad position, since Google's ad auction weighs both bid amount and predicted relevance/performance ? so improving Quality Score is often a more sustainable way to reduce costs than simply raising bids.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nQuality Score is Google Ads' estimate (on a 1-10 scale, shown at the keyword level) of how relevant and useful an ad, keyword, and landing page combination is likely to be to a searcher. It's influenced primarily by expected click-through rate, ad relevance to the keyword, and landing page experience (load speed, relevance, and usability). A higher Quality Score generally means a lower cost-per-click for the same ad position, since Google's ad auction weighs both bid amount and predicted relevance/performance ? so improving Quality Score is often a more sustainable way to reduce costs than simply raising bids.\n\n**Example:**\n\nTwo advertisers bid the same amount on a keyword, but Advertiser A has tightly-matched ad copy and a fast, relevant landing page (high Quality Score) while Advertiser B has generic ad copy pointing to a slow, unrelated page (low Quality Score). Advertiser A will typically pay less per click for the same or better ad position.",
