    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThe Search Network shows text ads directly within search engine results pages when someone searches a matching term ? it targets active, expressed intent. The Display Network shows visual (image/banner) or text ads across a large network of partner websites, apps, and video content, based on audience targeting, topics, or remarketing rather than an active search query ? it targets passive attention and broader reach rather than immediate intent. Search ads can appear beyond Google's own results pages if the advertiser opts into \"Search Partners,\" a network of other search-oriented sites that also show Google Search ads, which is a separate setting from the Display Network.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA search for \"emergency plumber near me\" would trigger a Search Network ad exactly at the moment of intent. A Display Network ad for the same plumbing company might instead appear as a banner on a local news website to someone who isn't actively searching, built for broader brand visibility rather than immediate conversion.",
