    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Bounce rate (in the classic Universal Analytics sense) is the percentage of sessions where a user viewed only one page and took no further tracked interaction before leaving; in GA...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you understand how search visibility and traffic acquisition work in practice, rather than relying on isolated SEO terminology.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- Explain the concept accurately and in terms of search intent or relevance.\n- Recognize the practical trade-offs and common implementation mistakes.\n- Connect the tactic to a realistic website or campaign decision.",
    sampleAnswer: "Bounce rate (in the classic Universal Analytics sense) is the percentage of sessions where a user viewed only one page and took no further tracked interaction before leaving; in GA4, the related concept is inverted into \"engagement rate,\" which counts a session as engaged if it lasts a meaningful duration, has a conversion event, or includes multiple page/screen views. A high bounce/low-engagement rate often signals a mismatch between what brought the user to the page (an ad, a search result) and what the page actually delivers, slow load time, poor mobile experience, or a confusing layout without a clear next step. Reducing it typically means aligning the landing page content tightly with the referring source's promise, improving page speed, and giving the visitor an obvious, low-friction next action.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nBounce rate (in the classic Universal Analytics sense) is the percentage of sessions where a user viewed only one page and took no further tracked interaction before leaving; in GA4, the related concept is inverted into \"engagement rate,\" which counts a session as engaged if it lasts a meaningful duration, has a conversion event, or includes multiple page/screen views. A high bounce/low-engagement rate often signals a mismatch between what brought the user to the page (an ad, a search result) and what the page actually delivers, slow load time, poor mobile experience, or a confusing layout without a clear next step. Reducing it typically means aligning the landing page content tightly with the referring source's promise, improving page speed, and giving the visitor an obvious, low-friction next action.\n\n**Example:**\n\nAn ad promising \"20% off winter jackets\" that lands users on a generic homepage (rather than a jackets category page with the discount visible) will likely see high bounce rates ? sending that traffic directly to a matching, discount-visible landing page usually reduces it noticeably.",
