    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you understand how search visibility and traffic acquisition work in practice, rather than relying on isolated SEO terminology.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- Explain the concept accurately and in terms of search intent or relevance.\n- Recognize the practical trade-offs and common implementation mistakes.\n- Connect the tactic to a realistic website or campaign decision.",
    sampleAnswer: "UTM parameters are tags appended to a URL (source, medium, campaign, and optionally term/content) that let analytics tools attribute traffic and conversions to the specific campaign, channel, or even individual ad creative that generated the click, rather than lumping it into a generic referral or direct-traffic bucket. They're used whenever traffic is driven from outside your own analytics-tracked ecosystem and you want campaign-level (not just channel-level) attribution ? email newsletters, social posts, paid ads on platforms that don't auto-tag, partner/affiliate links, and QR codes on offline materials. Consistent naming conventions matter, since inconsistent casing or naming (\"Newsletter\" vs. \"newsletter\") fragments the same campaign into multiple rows in reporting.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nUTM parameters are tags appended to a URL (source, medium, campaign, and optionally term/content) that let analytics tools attribute traffic and conversions to the specific campaign, channel, or even individual ad creative that generated the click, rather than lumping it into a generic referral or direct-traffic bucket. They're used whenever traffic is driven from outside your own analytics-tracked ecosystem and you want campaign-level (not just channel-level) attribution ? email newsletters, social posts, paid ads on platforms that don't auto-tag, partner/affiliate links, and QR codes on offline materials. Consistent naming conventions matter, since inconsistent casing or naming (\"Newsletter\" vs. \"newsletter\") fragments the same campaign into multiple rows in reporting.\n\n**Example:**\n\nhttps://example.com/sale?utm_source=newsletter&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=winter_sale_2026\nlets you see in Google Analytics exactly how much traffic and revenue came from that specific email campaign, separate from organic or other paid traffic.",
