    shortDescription: "First rule out a measurement or technical issue rather than assuming it's a ranking problem: check whether tracking/tagging broke, whether a recent site migration or robots.txt/noi...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to check whether you understand how search visibility and traffic acquisition work in practice, rather than relying on isolated SEO terminology.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- Explain the concept accurately and in terms of search intent or relevance.\n- Recognize the practical trade-offs and common implementation mistakes.\n- Connect the tactic to a realistic website or campaign decision.",
    sampleAnswer: "First rule out a measurement or technical issue rather than assuming it's a ranking problem: check whether tracking/tagging broke, whether a recent site migration or robots.txt/noindex change accidentally blocked pages from being crawled or indexed, and whether the drop is site-wide or limited to specific pages or query types. If tracking and indexing are fine, check Google Search Console for manual actions or algorithm-update timing correlated with the drop, review recent site changes (content removal, URL structure changes without proper redirects, a Core Web Vitals regression from a site redesign), and check whether competitors' content or backlink profiles changed for the affected queries. The investigation should move from \"is the traffic actually gone\" to \"is this a measurement, technical, content, or external competitive issue\" in that order, since jumping straight to a content rewrite when the real cause is a broken redirect wastes effort.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nFirst rule out a measurement or technical issue rather than assuming it's a ranking problem: check whether tracking/tagging broke, whether a recent site migration or robots.txt/noindex change accidentally blocked pages from being crawled or indexed, and whether the drop is site-wide or limited to specific pages or query types. If tracking and indexing are fine, check Google Search Console for manual actions or algorithm-update timing correlated with the drop, review recent site changes (content removal, URL structure changes without proper redirects, a Core Web Vitals regression from a site redesign), and check whether competitors' content or backlink profiles changed for the affected queries. The investigation should move from \"is the traffic actually gone\" to \"is this a measurement, technical, content, or external competitive issue\" in that order, since jumping straight to a content rewrite when the real cause is a broken redirect wastes effort.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA traffic drop that coincides exactly with a site migration to a new URL structure, without matching 301 redirects in place, is very likely a technical issue (lost link equity and broken indexing) rather than a content-quality or algorithm-penalty problem ? checking Search Console's coverage report for a spike in 404s or 'not indexed' pages would confirm this quickly.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n\nPAID ADVERTISING & PPC OPTIMIZATION\n\n",
