    shortDescription: "Don't assume causation just because the timing lines up with a publicized update ? check whether the performance drop is isolated to this account or reflects an industry-wide patte...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this to verify that you understand the digital-marketing concept and can apply it to a practical business situation.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- Explain the core concept accurately.\n- Discuss the most important practical trade-off or risk.\n- Use a concrete example to show how you would apply it.",
    sampleAnswer: "Don't assume causation just because the timing lines up with a publicized update ? check whether the performance drop is isolated to this account or reflects an industry-wide pattern (platform release notes, marketing forums, and peer benchmarks can help confirm this), and rule out account-specific causes first: a budget or bid change, a landing page or tracking change made around the same time, seasonality, or a competitor's aggressive bidding increase driving up auction pressure. If other advertisers in similar verticals report similar drops timed to the same update, that's stronger evidence the algorithm change is the actual cause, and the response then focuses on adapting to the new ranking/targeting behavior (adjusting bidding strategy, creative, or targeting) rather than trying to \"fix\" something that was never broken on the account's own side.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nDon't assume causation just because the timing lines up with a publicized update ? check whether the performance drop is isolated to this account or reflects an industry-wide pattern (platform release notes, marketing forums, and peer benchmarks can help confirm this), and rule out account-specific causes first: a budget or bid change, a landing page or tracking change made around the same time, seasonality, or a competitor's aggressive bidding increase driving up auction pressure. If other advertisers in similar verticals report similar drops timed to the same update, that's stronger evidence the algorithm change is the actual cause, and the response then focuses on adapting to the new ranking/targeting behavior (adjusting bidding strategy, creative, or targeting) rather than trying to \"fix\" something that was never broken on the account's own side.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA campaign's conversion rate drops right after a reported Meta algorithm update. Checking whether a landing page redesign shipped in the same week, whether tracking pixel events are still firing correctly, and whether similar advertisers in the same industry reported the same drop would all help isolate whether the algorithm change, an internal change, or a tracking issue is the real cause.",
