    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "The basic formula is (Revenue attributable to the campaign ? Campaign cost) / Campaign cost, expressed as a percentage or ratio. The common mistakes that distort this: attributing...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this because digital marketers need to distinguish activity metrics from genuine business impact and understand the limits of attribution.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- Choose metrics that match the business objective.\n- Identify measurement bias, attribution limitations, or tracking gaps.\n- Explain how measurement would influence optimization or budget decisions.",
    sampleAnswer: "The basic formula is (Revenue attributable to the campaign ? Campaign cost) / Campaign cost, expressed as a percentage or ratio. The common mistakes that distort this: attributing all revenue from a conversion to the last channel a customer touched (ignoring earlier channels that contributed), failing to include the full cost of the campaign (not just media spend, but also creative production, tools, and staff time), and confusing revenue with profit ? a campaign can show impressive ROI on revenue while being unprofitable once cost of goods sold is factored in, especially for lower-margin products.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nThe basic formula is (Revenue attributable to the campaign ? Campaign cost) / Campaign cost, expressed as a percentage or ratio. The common mistakes that distort this: attributing all revenue from a conversion to the last channel a customer touched (ignoring earlier channels that contributed), failing to include the full cost of the campaign (not just media spend, but also creative production, tools, and staff time), and confusing revenue with profit ? a campaign can show impressive ROI on revenue while being unprofitable once cost of goods sold is factored in, especially for lower-margin products.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA campaign spends $10,000 and is credited with $10,000 in attributable revenue on a full-price product with 40% gross margin. Naive revenue-based ROI would look breakeven, but the actual gross profit generated ($4,000) is less than the spend ? meaning the campaign was unprofitable even though the revenue-to-spend ratio looked acceptable on the surface.",
    keyPoints: ["Choose metrics that match the business objective.", "Identify measurement bias, attribution limitations, or tracking gaps.", "Explain how measurement would influence optimization or budget decisions."],
