    interviewType: "TECHNICAL",
    shortDescription: "Common attribution models include last-click (100% credit to the final touchpoint before conversion ? simple but overvalues bottom-funnel channels like branded search and retargeti...",
    explanation: "Interviewers ask this because digital marketers need to distinguish activity metrics from genuine business impact and understand the limits of attribution.\n\nWhat the interviewer wants:\n- Choose metrics that match the business objective.\n- Identify measurement bias, attribution limitations, or tracking gaps.\n- Explain how measurement would influence optimization or budget decisions.",
    sampleAnswer: "Common attribution models include last-click (100% credit to the final touchpoint before conversion ? simple but overvalues bottom-funnel channels like branded search and retargeting), first-click (credit to the first touchpoint ? overvalues awareness channels), linear (equal credit across all touchpoints), time-decay (more credit to touchpoints closer to conversion), and data-driven attribution (uses actual conversion path data and modeling to assign credit based on each touchpoint's real contribution, generally the most accurate but requiring enough data volume to be reliable). The right choice depends on typical customer journey length and the decision the attribution is informing ? last-click is a poor basis for deciding whether to invest in upper-funnel awareness spend, since it structurally can't give credit to touchpoints that aren't the final click.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer:**\n\nCommon attribution models include last-click (100% credit to the final touchpoint before conversion ? simple but overvalues bottom-funnel channels like branded search and retargeting), first-click (credit to the first touchpoint ? overvalues awareness channels), linear (equal credit across all touchpoints), time-decay (more credit to touchpoints closer to conversion), and data-driven attribution (uses actual conversion path data and modeling to assign credit based on each touchpoint's real contribution, generally the most accurate but requiring enough data volume to be reliable). The right choice depends on typical customer journey length and the decision the attribution is informing ? last-click is a poor basis for deciding whether to invest in upper-funnel awareness spend, since it structurally can't give credit to touchpoints that aren't the final click.\n\n**Example:**\n\nA customer sees a display ad (awareness), later clicks a retargeting ad, and finally converts via a branded search click. Last-click attribution gives 100% of the credit to branded search, making the earlier display and retargeting touchpoints look worthless ? even though the branded search click may not have happened at all without the earlier exposure that built awareness and consideration.",
    keyPoints: ["Choose metrics that match the business objective.", "Identify measurement bias, attribution limitations, or tracking gaps.", "Explain how measurement would influence optimization or budget decisions."],
